From: Aegir <dev@aegir.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] clang: default riscv64-unknown-elf to Aegir's pinned ABI

The build compiles everything with the pinned ABI spelled out
(`-march=rv64imafdc_zicsr_zifencei -mabi=lp64d`, specs/build.md), so a
program the on-device `aegir-clang` compiles for `riscv64-unknown-elf`
with no `-march`/`-mabi` must agree with the build's own objects. Upstream
defaults a bare-metal riscv64 target to soft-float `rv64imac`/`lp64`, which
is what made the acceptance's `hello.o` refuse to link against the staged
`crt0.o` (hard-float `lp64d`).

The ABI default is normally derived from the arch string
(`getRISCVABI` step 2), so the arch default is the load-bearing change; the
triple fallback is made explicit for the same reason the build is.

diff --git a/clang/lib/Driver/ToolChains/Arch/RISCV.cpp b/clang/lib/Driver/ToolChains/Arch/RISCV.cpp
index 6935904a24ed..0e808a3ea763 100644
--- a/clang/lib/Driver/ToolChains/Arch/RISCV.cpp
+++ b/clang/lib/Driver/ToolChains/Arch/RISCV.cpp
@@ -240,7 +240,7 @@ StringRef riscv::getRISCVABI(const ArgList &Args, const llvm::Triple &Triple) {
       return "ilp32d";
   } else {
     if (Triple.getOS() == llvm::Triple::UnknownOS)
-      return "lp64";
+      return "lp64d";
     else
       return "lp64d";
   }
@@ -342,7 +342,7 @@ std::string riscv::getRISCVArch(const llvm::opt::ArgList &Args,
       return "rv32imafdc";
   } else {
     if (Triple.getOS() == llvm::Triple::UnknownOS)
-      return "rv64imac";
+      return "rv64imafdc_zicsr_zifencei";
     else if (Triple.isAndroid())
       return "rv64imafdcv_zba_zbb_zbs";
     else
